PATCH 199: the FUN_0000_d501 FSG-parse terminator flags a974/a975 are BYTEs, not WORDs
(store-width / paired-16-bit typing class; the ROOT of the "parse stops after SHDR" blocker).

Exposed once patches 197/198 fixed the d501 chunk-header read + type-dispatch so the SHDR chunk
finally MATCHED and dispatched its handler d7b5.  Empirically (shim AZER1.FSG handle trace) the
parser then read ONLY the 6-byte SHDR magic + the 54-byte SHDR body and immediately CLOSED the file
at pos=60 -- it never advanced to DCBS/PATH/STMP/PINF/BINF/TERM, so the map-name block (BINF) was
never read, 63b8 never staged the map names, and D32.KLC/C32.KLC/532.pal/5.SKY never loaded.

ROOT CAUSE (asm-verified vs re_out/fist_dat_image.bin): the FSG loop-terminator flag DGROUP:0xe974
(DAT_2000_a974) and the SHDR-empty flag DGROUP:0xe975 (DAT_2000_a975) are BYTE flags in every access:
  d516  c6 06 74 e9 00   mov  BYTE PTR [0xe974],0     (d501: clear terminator)
  d57e  80 3e 74 e9 00   cmp  BYTE PTR [0xe974],0     (d501: loop-exit test -> je read-next-chunk)
  d7da  c6 06 74 e9 01   mov  BYTE PTR [0xe974],1     (TERM handler: set terminator)
  d7c7  c6 06 75 e9 01   mov  BYTE PTR [0xe975],1     (d7b5 SHDR: a982==0 flag)
  d7d3  c6 06 75 e9 00   mov  BYTE PTR [0xe975],0
  d87f/d8a9/d8e9  80 3e 75 e9 01  cmp BYTE PTR [0xe975],1   (PATH/PINF/STMP readers)
Ghidra typed BOTH as `undefined2` (WORD).  Because they are ADJACENT (0xe974 / 0xe975), the WORD
DAT_2000_a974 spans bytes [0xe974..0xe975] and the WORD DAT_2000_a975 spans [0xe975..0xe976] --
overlapping each other AND the chunk-type low byte a976.  Sequence that breaks the parse:
  1. d501 sets byte[0xe974]=0 (via the WORD store, high byte = byte[0xe975] left intact).
  2. SHDR handler d7b5 executes `DAT_2000_a975 = DAT_2000_a982 == 0`.  The SHDR body's first word is
     0 (verified: buf[0..3]=00000000), so a975 = 1 -- but the WORD store writes 0x0001 to
     [0xe975..0xe976], i.e. byte[0xe975] = 1.
  3. d501's terminator test `if (DAT_2000_a974 != 0)` reads the WORD [0xe974..0xe975] = (byte[0xe975]
     << 8) | byte[0xe974] = 0x0100 != 0 -> the loop FINALIZES after SHDR instead of continuing.
The byte flag is 0 (correct); the spurious high byte from the overlapping a975 makes the WORD nonzero.

FIX: type both macros `undefined1` (BYTE), matching every asm access.  All C uses are already
byte-valued (`= '\0'`, `!= '\0'`, `= '\x01'`, `== '\x01'`) so no site changes.  With this, a974 stays
0 across SHDR/DCBS/PATH/STMP/PINF/BINF and is set to 1 only by the TERM handler d7da -> d501 parses
the whole scenario file.  VERIFIED (shim AZER1.FSG trace, FIST_CASCADE 3-click route): the parse now
advances SHDR(6+54) -> DCBS header(6) -> DCBS sub-records, reaching the DCBS unit-database handler
(d7e1->d81e).  Behaviour-neutral for the 19 covered flows: a974/a975 are touched only inside the FSG
chunk parser (d501 + the chunk handlers), which is off the menu/dialog paths (verify 19/19).
--- a/fist.c
+++ b/fist.c
@@ -3646,8 +3646,8 @@
 #define DAT_2000_a96e (*(undefined2 *)(g_mem+0x2a96e))
 #define DAT_2000_a970 (*(undefined2 *)(g_mem+0x2a970))
 #define DAT_2000_a972 (*(undefined2 *)(g_mem+0x2a972))
-#define DAT_2000_a974 (*(undefined2 *)(g_mem+0x2a974))
-#define DAT_2000_a975 (*(undefined2 *)(g_mem+0x2a975))
+#define DAT_2000_a974 (*(undefined1 *)(g_mem+0x2a974))
+#define DAT_2000_a975 (*(undefined1 *)(g_mem+0x2a975))
 #define DAT_2000_a976 (*(undefined2 *)(g_mem+0x2a976))
